# Untitled

Photocatalytic $H_{2}$ evolution improvement for H free-radical stabilization by electrostatic interaction of a Cu-BTC MOF with ZnO/GO
Xifeng Shi, Jiahui Zhang, Guanwei Cui $:\boxtimes)$ , Ning Deng, Wen Wang, Qian Wang, and Bo Tang $(\boxtimes)$
College of Chemistry, Chemical Engineering and Materials Science, Collaborative Innovation Center of Functionalized Probes for Chemical Imaging, Key Laboratory of Molecular and Nano Probes, Ministry of Education, Shandong Normal University, Jinan 250014, China
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2017
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic framework,   
free radical stabilization,   
$\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution,   
photocatalysis,   
electrostatic interaction   
assembly
## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are self-assembled molecular containers that can encapsulate and stabilize short-lived reaction intermediates. In this study, the Cu-benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (BTC) MOF was incorporated in a $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}/$ graphene oxide (GO) photocatalytic system by electrostatic interaction, and the obtained assembly showed improved hydrogen evolution activity. Electron spin resonance analysis was used to detect and monitor free radicals in the photocatalytic system, and demonstrated that Cu-BTC MOF could stabilize and extend the lifetime of free radicals, increasing the chance of H· radical recombination to form $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . This work provides a new strategy for designing highly efficient photocatalysts.
## 1 Introduction
In the past decades, great efforts have been made to enhance photocatalytic reactions such as water splitting and carbon dioxide reduction with water. Recent studies have mostly focused on the development of photocatalysts with extended light-harvesting range and increased electron–hole separation efficiency. However, as most photocatalytic reactions proceed through free radical intermediates, the important role of separated electrons in photochemical processes should also be considered in the design of new photocatalysts. For instance, numerous studies have proven the existence of hydroxyl radicals in solutions of ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ which is the most commonly used photocatalytic material [1–3]. Light-induced radical generation provides unique temporal control of reactions; however, the lifetime of radicals is usually much shorter than the reaction rate. Thus, we envisioned that the introduction of a functional unit that could extend the lifetime of free radicals in a photocatalytic system would effectively improve the photocatalytic performance.
As mentioned above, effective electron–hole separation and electron transfer, life extension of photogenerated intermediates, and effective photogeneration of electrons are key parameters for improving the photocatalytic activity. Graphene oxide (GO) is known to have a beneficial effect on the electron–hole separation. Moreover, it has been reported that selfassembled metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can serve as molecular containers for encapsulation and stabilization of short-lived intermediates [4–10]. Thus, the combination of GO and MOFs is expected to improve the photocatalytic performance. In particular, the investigation of the influence of MOFs on the stabilization of free radicals generated by light irradiation could contribute to the development of a new approach.
Graphene is a $\pi$ -system decorated with abundant oxygen-containing functional groups such as epoxy, hydroxyl, and carboxyl groups [11], which are involved in $\pi{-}\pi,\ \mathrm{H-}\pi,$ and ionic– $\cdot\pi$ interactions with other $\pi$ -systems [12, 13]. These combined $\pi$ -interaction effects could meet the requirements of maximum electron transfer distance, which is the sum of the depletion width and effective minority carrier diffusion length of a collector [14]. In this paper, Cu-benzene-1,3,5- tricarboxylate (BTC) $(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3})$ MOF was introduced into the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ photocatalyst by electrostatic interaction assembly to stabilize the $\mathrm{H\cdot}$ radicals generated from the photoreduction reaction. In the proposed structure shown in Scheme 1, $Z\mathrm{nO}$ served as the photoelectron donor, and GO as the photoelectron-transfer channel from $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ to $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC and as the supporting matrix for $Z\mathrm{nO}$ and Cu-BTC. Cu-BTC was expected to serve as a microcontainer for the free radicals generated by light irradiation to extend their lifetime. For comparison, the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}/(C u-B T C)/C O}}$ complex was prepared by the substep hydrothermal method. The photocatalytic efficiency of the electrostatic interaction assembly of Cu-BTC and $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution was investigated, and improved activity was observed.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Material synthesis
## 2.1.1 Preparation of GO
GO was prepared from purified natural graphite powder according to a reported method [15].
## 2.1.2 Preparation of ZnO/GO
Spherical $Z\mathrm{nO}$ aggregates were loaded on GO according to a previously reported method with some improvements [16]. Briefly, $0.11\mathrm{g}$ zinc acetate dihydrate $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ was dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ diethylene glycol. After stirring at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for about $^{\mathrm{1h,}}$ the solution was cooled to room temperature. Next, $10\mathrm{mg}$ GO was dispersed in $20\mathrm{mL}$ of diethylene glycol under continuous ultrasound irradiation, and mixed with the above diethylene glycol solution of $Z{\mathrm{n}}({\mathrm{Ac}})_{2}$ . The mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The obtained residues were thoroughly rinsed with distilled water and absolute ethanol by centrifugation. The final products were dried under vacuum for later use.
## 2.1.3 Preparation of Cu-BTC
$\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC MOF aggregates were prepared by an improved in situ synthesis method of Cu-BTC reported in the literature [17–19]. Briefly, a solution of $0.2\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $40\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water was homogeneously mixed with a solution of $0.1\mathrm{g}$ of trimesic acid in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol. The mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . The obtained residues were thoroughly rinsed with distilled water and absolute ethanol by centrifugation. The final $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC products were dried under vacuum for later use.
## 2.1.4 Preparation of (Cu-BTC)/GO
Cu-BTC MOF aggregates anchored on GO were prepared by an improved in situ synthesis method of $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC. Briefly, a solution of $0.2\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $40\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water (containing $4\mathrm{mg}$ of GO) was homogeneously mixed with a solution of $0.1\mathrm{g}$ of trimesic acid in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol. The mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The obtained residues were thoroughly rinsed with distilled water and absolute ethanol by centrifugation. The final products were dried under vacuum for later use.
## 2.1.5 Electrostatic interaction assembly of ZnO/GO and Cu-BTC
A dispersion of $0.1\mathrm{g}$ of as-prepared $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ was mixed with a dispersion of $0.025\mathrm{g}$ of as-prepared $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The mixture was continuously stirred for $24\mathrm{h},$ centrifuged, and dried under vacuum for later use.
## 2.1.6 Preparation of $Z n O/(C u–B T C)/G O$
First, $0.22{\mathrm{g}}$ of $Z n(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dispersed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of diethylene glycol and heated to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain the solution. After cooling to room temperature, $15\mathrm{mg}$ of as-prepared $(\mathrm{Cu-BTC})/\mathrm{GO}$ was homogeneously dispersed in the solution by ultrasound irradiation. The mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . The obtained residues were thoroughly rinsed with distilled water and absolute ethanol by centrifugation. The final $\mathrm{ZnO/(Cu{\mathrm{-}}B T C)/G O}$ products were dried under vacuum for later use.
## 2.2 Material characterization
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were taken on a JEM-2100 electron microscope (JEOL, Japan) with an accelerating voltage of $120\mathrm{kV}.$ Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) data were collected on polycrystalline products mounted on Si (111) zero background sample holders using a D8 Advance diffractometer (Bruker, Germany) with $\operatorname{Cu}\ K\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=1.5406\mathring\mathrm{A})$ with a step width of $0.02^{\circ}$ in the $2\theta$ range from $5^{\circ}$ to $70^{\circ}$ . The scan speed, applied voltage, and current were $0.2\ ^{\circ}{\cdot}s^{-1}.$ , $40\mathrm{kV,}$ and $40\mathrm{mA},$ , respectively.
## 2.3 Electron spin resonance (ESR) measurements
Sample preparation: Samples obtained from the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution reaction were subjected to ESR analysis. First, $20\mathrm{mg}$ of photocatalyst powder was dispersed in $10\mathrm{mL}$ of double-distilled water containing 2-methyl-N-(4-pyridinylmethylene)-2-propanamine $\mathrm{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dioxide (POBN) $(1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{\cdotmL}^{-1})$ , and the mixture was thoroughly deaerated by bubbling argon for $60\mathrm{min}$ with continuous stirring prior to light irradiation. Next, the mixture was placed in a quartz tube sealed with a rubber plug, and was irradiated with a 500-W mercury lamp (the light intensity irradiating the photocatalysts was 0.020 W) with continuous stirring. It should be mentioned that sacrificial reagents were not added to the photocatalytic system, and the quartz tube was not opened until centrifugation and ESR analysis. If the same sample is to be used for kinetics experiments, it was sealed in different quartz tubes for different irradiation times.
ESR measurements: POBN, used as a spin trap, was purchased from Alfa Aesar (Beijing, China) and used without further purification. After irradiation, the prepared samples were centrifuged, and the supernatant liquid was transferred to a quartz capillary for immediate ESR analysis performed with a Bruker A300-10/12/S-LC spectrometer using a $100\mathrm{-kHz}$ modulation frequency.
## 2.4 Hydrogen evolution test
The photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution experiments were performed in a $20\mathrm{-mL}$ Pyrex bottle sealed with a silicone rubber septum, at ambient temperature and pressure. Hydrogen production was performed by dispersing the catalyst powder containing $20\mathrm{mg}$ of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ active sites in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of double-distilled water containing methanol $(10\mathrm{vol\%})$ ) as a sacrificial electron donor. The sample solutions were thoroughly deaerated by bubbling argon for $30\mathrm{min}$ prior to the photocatalysis experiment. Each sample was irradiated with a 500-W mercury lamp (the light intensity irradiating the photocatalysts was 0.020 W) at room temperature with continuous stirring. The evolved gases were analyzed by gas chromatography (FULI 9750, thermal conductivity detector, argon as the carrier gas, $5\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ molecular sieve column). The sample was analyzed by injecting $1\mathrm{mL}$ of headspace gas into the gas chromatograph, and quantified using an external standard calibration plot.
## 2.5 Photoelectrochemical measurements
Photocurrent was measured on an electrochemical analyzer (CHI660D Instruments) in a standard threeelectrode system using the prepared samples as the working electrodes with an active area of about $2.0\mathrm{cm}^{2},$ , a Pt sheet as the counter electrode, and a saturated calomel electrode as a reference electrode. The working electrodes were prepared as follows: $0.05\mathrm{g}$ of the sample was grinded with $0.1\mathrm{g}$ terpinol to form a slurry. Next, the slurry was coated onto a $4\mathrm{cm}\times1\mathrm{cm}$ indium tin oxide-coated glass electrode by the doctor blade technique. The electrode was dried in vacuum and calcined at $280^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ under Ar.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and characterization
The morphology of the as-prepared $Z{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ and $(\mathrm{Cu-BTC})/\mathrm{GO},$ and the structure of the electrostatic interaction assembly of $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC and of the $\mathrm{ZnO/(Cu{\mathrm{-}}B T C)/G O}$ complex were characterized by
TEM. As shown in Fig. 1, ZnO (Fig. 1(a)) and Cu-BTC (Fig. 1(b)) were uniformly loaded on GO. The structure of the electrostatic interaction assembly of Cu-BTC and $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ (Fig. 1(c)) was very similar to that of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}/(C u-B T C)/C O}}$ complex (Fig. 1(d)) prepared by the substep method, indicating that Cu-BTC was successfully assembled on $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ . Moreover, the diffraction peaks of $Z\mathrm{nO,}$ Cu-BTC, and GO were all found in the XRD spectra (Fig. 2) of both the electrostatic interaction assembly of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC and $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ and the $Z_{\mathrm{nO/(Cu-BTC)/GO}}$ complex, indicating the successful modification of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ and Cu-BTC on GO by the electrostatic interaction assembly method.
The assembly of Cu-BTC on $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ mainly resulted from the electrostatic interaction between Cu-BTC and $Z{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ . As shown in Fig. 3, when dispersed in water, $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC had a zeta potential of $33.4\mathrm{mV}$ (Fig. 3(a)) and $-4.06\mathrm{mV}$ (Fig. 3(b)), respectively. This indicated that $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC and $Z{\mathrm{nO}}/{\mathrm{GO}}$ had opposite surface charges, which contributed to their assembly by electrostatic attraction.
## 3.2 ESR characterization
As reported in the literature, MOFs can encapsulate and stabilize short-lived intermediates. To investigate the unique role of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC in photocatalytic systems, a photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution experiment was used as the model reaction, and ESR measurements were performed to detect and monitor free radicals during the reaction. First, $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ was used as the photocatalyst with continuous Ar bubbling and without sacrificial reagent, and the formation of free radical species in the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution system was evaluated. As shown in Fig. 4(a), $3\times3$ lines were observed in the ESR spectrum due to the H-POBN adduct, as reported in the literature, suggesting that H· radicals were formed in $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ photocatalytic systems under UV light irradiation for ${\mathrm{4h}},$ and were captured by the POBN spin trap [20]. Interestingly, no H-POBN adduct ESR signal was detected in the spectrum of the (Cu-BTC)/GO photocatalytic system (Fig. 4(b)), indicating that the H· radicals in the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ system did not result from GO but only from $Z\mathrm{nO,}$ and the introduction of $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC MOF did not affect the ESR spectrum. Thus, the ESR measurements not only demonstrated the formation of H· during the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution process but also proved to be an effective approach to characterize the kinetics of free radicals in photocatalytic reactions.
To investigate the effect of the addition of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC MOF to the $Z{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution system on the stabilization of $\mathrm{H\cdot}$ radicals, ESR measurements of $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ and composites of $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ and Cu-BTC containing the same amount of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ were conducted simultaneously. Samples were taken every $2\mathrm{h}$ of UV irradiation and centrifuged, and the supernatant was immediately analyzed by ESR. As shown in Fig. 5, the relative ESR signal intensity of the electrostatic interaction assembly increased with UV irradiation time, and the $3\times3$ lines of the H-POBN adduct remained unchanged even after $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ .
The relative ESR signal intensity of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ also increased with irradiation time, but after $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ the spectrum showed $2\times3$ splitting lines and not the $3\times3$ splitting lines of the H-POBN adduct (Fig. 6), suggesting that after 4-h UV irradiation H· free radicals were no longer present in the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ photocatalytic system.
ZnO may leach under UV light irradiation; however, because POBN is reported to be a trapping agent for ·H, ·D, ·COOH, and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{CHOH}$ radicals [20], the $2\times3$ splitting lines may derive from ·COOH trapping by POBN due to leaching of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and not $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ in the photocatalytic equipment. Moreover, in the first $\mathsf{4h},$ the relative H-POBN ESR signal intensity of the assembled complex was higher than that of $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ (Fig. 7).
These results demonstrate that $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC MOF could stabilize and extend the lifetime of free radicals, increasing the chances of H· recombination to form $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and thus improving the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution efficiency.
## 3.3 Photocatalytic properties
The ESR results indicate that the H· radical-stabilizing effect of the $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC electrostatic interaction assembly on $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}}$ might improve the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution efficiency by enabling H· radical recombination reactions to form $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . To further investigate the contribution of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC MOF to the photocatalytic efficiency, photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution experiments were conducted using ZnO, Cu-BTC, $Z{\mathrm{nO/GO}},$ , (CuBTC)/GO, electrostatic interaction assembly of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC, and $Z\mathrm{nO}/(\mathrm{Cu}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{BTC})/\mathrm{GO}$ complex as photocatalysts. As shown in Fig. 8, upon UV irradiation of a dispersion of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC or $(\mathrm{Cu-BTC})/\mathrm{GO}$ alone in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , no $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution was observed. Notably, the electrostatic interaction assembly exhibited the highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate $(191\ \mathrm{\upmumol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}})$ , which was about nine times that of $Z{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ $(21\upmu\mathrm{mol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}})$ and three times that of the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}/(C u-B T C)/C O}}$ complex $(58\upmu\mathrm{mol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}})$ . However, photocurrent density measurements (Fig. 9) showed that $Z{\mathrm{{nO/GO}}}$ exhibited the highest photocurrent, whereas a small change in photocurrent was observed for $(\mathrm{Cu-BTC})/\mathrm{GO}$ . These findings suggest that the improved photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution of the electrostatic interaction assembly did not result from an increase in photoelectron production of Cu-BTC or $Z\mathrm{nO},$ , but was rather due to the H· radical-stabilizing effect of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC, which increased the chance of $\mathrm{H\cdot}$ recombination to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ as demonstrated by ESR analysis. Moreover, the photocurrent density measurements showed that the electrostatic interaction assembly generated more photoelectrons than the $Z_{\mathrm{nO/(Cu-BTC)/GO}}$ complex, which also contributed to the improved photocatalytic efficiency, in synergy with the H·-stabilizing effect of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC.
## 3.4 Stability experiments
In order to evaluate the stability of the photocatalyst, the XRD pattern of the recycled photocatalyst was compared with that of the fresh composite, as shown in Fig. 10. The main diffraction peak of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ remained unchanged, whereas changes were detected for some diffraction peaks of Cu-BTC, indicating that $Z\mathrm{nO}$ was stable in the photocatalytic system, and the Cu-BTC framework still existed but with different crystallinity.
In a previous study, $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC was reported to lose crystallinity in the presence of water because of the coordination of water molecules to free copper sites [21], which explains the crystallinity change of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC observed in this study. However, this did not affect the important role of Cu-BTC in stabilizing free radicals, as shown by the ESR results (Fig. 5), suggesting the possible universality of MOF materials for stabilizing free radicals in photocatalytic reactions.
## 3.5 Proposed mechanism of $\mathbf{{Cu}}$ -BTC in photocatalytic activity improvement
On the basis of the ESR results, which suggested the formation of H· radicals during the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution reaction, and the role of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC MOF in stabilizing and extending the lifetime of these radicals, two routes are proposed for the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution, as shown in Scheme 1. In the first route, a $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ ion was reduced to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with formation of two photoelectrons accumulated on the conduction band or transferred to GO. In the other route, one photoelectron from $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ was transferred to GO with formation of an H· radical, which was encapsulated in the $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC MOF channel and stabilized. Thus, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution efficiency was improved due to the increased probability of H· recombination to form $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules.
## 4 Conclusions
In this work, the Cu-BTC MOF was incorporated into a photocatalytic system by electrostatic interaction, and its beneficial effect on the photocatalytic activity was investigated using photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution as the model reaction. ESR analysis showed that the improved photocatalytic efficiency resulted from the
H· radical-stabilizing effect of Cu-BTC. This work provides a new strategy to achieve high solar energy conversion efficiency, solving the bottleneck problem of photocatalysis research.
